# Turn a monster's gaze attack back on it, and read the sentence the game
# prints about it (bd inc-upw.30).
#
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/gaze-reflect-message.keys 4
#
# WHAT IS BEING MEASURED. src/Magic.cpp:941, inside Magic::MagicStrike, answers
# a gaze effect aimed at a creature carrying GAZE_REFLECTION / GR_REFLECT by
# turning the effect around and printing, to everyone but the gazer:
#
#     "The <Obj1>'s gaze is reflected back at <him:Obj1>!"
#
# Both tags name argument 1, and argument 1 is the only argument the call
# supplies. Before the fix both tags were unnumbered, and __XPrint reads an
# unnumbered obj/mon/itm tag as "take the NEXT vararg", so the second one read
# past the end of the list. That sentence is this script's oracle.
#
# WHY A BODAK, and how it keeps the two reflection sites apart. There are TWO
# places that answer a reflected gaze. src/Fight.cpp:1868 handles the A_GAZE
# entry in a monster's attack table and prints "The <EActor>'s gaze is
# reflected!". src/Magic.cpp:941 handles an EF_GAZE magical effect and prints
# the sentence above. A bodak (lib/mon3.irh:3422) has no A_GAZE attack at all --
# its attack table is A_SLAM and A_SEEM -- so the Fight.cpp branch cannot run
# for it. Its one spell, "Bodak Death Gaze" (lib/mon3.irh:3448), is one of only
# two EF_GAZE effects in lib/, so every reflection this session sees comes from
# Magic.cpp. The two sentences differ in words as well, which is the second way
# to tell them apart: only the Magic.cpp one says "back at".
#
# The bodak also announces itself. Its MSG_CAST prints "eyes glow with a black
# aura", so the screen says the gaze was cast as well as that it came back.
#
# WHY A MAGE, AND FIVE LEVELS. "Gaze Reflection" (lib/wspells.irh:2004) is a
# Mage 2 spell, and it is how the player gets the stati. Learn Any Spell teaches
# it to any class, but the Spell Manager will not cast what the character has no
# slot for, so the character needs a second-level slot. Four free levels also
# carry him through the bodak's slams; a bodak is CR 8-10 and the character is
# not meant to win, only to be gazed at. Cheat Death covers the rest.

@include chargen-mage.keys

# chargen-mage.keys stops on the arrival room description. Dismiss it, or the
# [w] below is eaten by the description.
ESC

# Wizard switches, counted from the top of the list in src/Tables.cpp:2674:
#   1 Cheat Death, 3 Free Advancement, 6 Ignore Spell Failure,
#   8 Infinite Mana, 10 Wizard Sight. Freeze Monsters is deliberately NOT set:
# the bodak has to take its turn and cast.
w
y
@choose "Wizard Mode Switches"
SPACE
DOWN*2
SPACE
DOWN*3
SPACE
DOWN*2
SPACE
DOWN*2
SPACE
ESC

# Four free levels, on the character screen. 'd' opens it and 'G' gains a level
# inside it. The three @while guards answer the three prompts a mage meets on
# the way up; the metamagic one arrives at level five and is easy to forget,
# and a level-up prompt left unanswered swallows every key after it.
#
# SPACE, not ESC, leaves the character screen. ESC is a bound command there and
# the screen simply redraws; the loop at src/Managers.cpp:2287 exits on any key
# it does not handle, and SPACE is one.
d
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
SPACE

# Teach the mage the one spell this fixture needs. Know All Spells is not used:
# it fills the Spell Manager with pages of spells and @choose reads only what is
# on the screen.
w
@choose "Learn Any Spell"
"Gaze Reflection"
ENTER

# Cast it on the caster himself. "*" targets self.
m
@choose "Gaze Reflection"
*
@expect "A reflective sheen covers your vision."

# The character sheet's Specials column, as evidence that the stati is really on
# the character before any gaze arrives. src/Sheet.cpp:653 is what prints it.
d
DOWN*20
@dump:sheet-specials
SPACE

# Summon the bodak three squares east, inside its bolt range and inside sight.
w
@choose "Monster Summoning"
@choose "Outsiders"
@choose "bodak"
RIGHT*3
ENTER
@dump:summoned

# Stand still and be gazed at. Each '.' is one turn; the bodak is faster than
# the character and casts on the first of them. The "Die?" guard is Cheat
# Death's prompt, which a script must answer or every later key is spent on it.
.
@while "Die?" y
@dump:wait01
.
@while "Die?" y
@dump:wait02
.
@while "Die?" y
@dump:wait03
.
@while "Die?" y
@dump:wait04
.
@while "Die?" y
@dump:wait05
.
@while "Die?" y
@dump:wait06
.
@while "Die?" y
@dump:wait07
.
@while "Die?" y
@dump:wait08

# The message log, which holds the sentence on one line of its own rather than
# wrapped into the two-line message area.
v
@dump:msglog0
PGUP
@dump:msglog1
@while "Messages" ESC
@quit
